        ]
      },
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta &=(4a+3b)^2-4a(-5a-3b)\\\\\\Delta &=36a^2+36ab+9b^2\\\\\\Delta &=9(2a+b)^2.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus $\\sqrt{\\Delta}=|3(2a+b)|$ is rational. The two signs in the quadratic formula allow $\\pm\\sqrt{\\Delta}$ to be written as $\\pm3(2a+b)$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\frac{-(4a+3b)\\pm3(2a+b)}{2a}\\\\x&=1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=-5-\\frac{3b}{a}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nBoth roots are rational because $a$ and $b$ are rational and $a\\ne0$.",
